it's early morning in port-au-prince and  worshipers dressed in their sunday best  are on their way to church  [Music]  as a wave of violent crime grips the  city many are praying for divine  intervention against criminal gangs who  are fighting for control over haiti's  capital  religious leaders tell us even places of  worship have become a target for  kidnappers  we know we're a church that has been  exposed because we are right there on  the streets  a week ago they tried to kidnap a member  of our church by the grace of god she  ran so we all have been affected by what  is going on in the country  so far this year at least 40 church  members have been kidnapped in haiti  this includes 17 missionaries associated  with a us-based christian charity who  were abducted last weekend by a group  called 400 malozo one of the city's most  dangerous gangs  prayer services across town now count on  added security measures like barbed wire  fences and even metal detectors to  screen for danger  this street in downtown port-au-prince  leads to a church and on sunday mornings  security guards block off the exits  using motor vehicles and they keep a  close watch on everyone who crosses the  ropes the efforts are to prevent  kidnappers from driving up to the doors  of the church and abducting the pastor  in just the first eight months of the  year close to 800 people were kidnapped  in the country and the un is pointing to  another alarming trend a dramatic  increase in the number of women and  children being targeted  kidnappings can take place at any given  time of the day or the night and  anywhere it sometimes takes place at  home in the street at school or even in  churches so there is no safe space for  children  anymore and we really see that the  criminal gangs are using children  increasingly as bargaining chips  it's an increasingly lucrative business  in the hands of the criminal gangs  this man who has asked us to hide his  identity is a kidnapping survivor he  tells al jazeera that he still lives  with the trauma of the experience and  worries that it could happen again as  insecurity continues to grow i'm scared  right now for my son because they come  into the yard and pick up the person  inside your yard now right now i don't  have to take the car out for them to get  nothing  i don't have to take my car out to go  against nothing they could come in here  more than a hundred abductions have been  recorded in the country in october alone  haiti currently has the highest  kidnapping rate in the world per capita  many here say violence has grown beyond  the ability for authorities to control  and that the last place they have left  to find solace is in the mercy of god  manuel rapalo al-jazeera port-au-prince
 

